  "minutes": 22,
  "title": {
    "en": "Eclipse drakes and sawbills"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Males in disguise, and the fish-eating ducks with saw-toothed bills"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "In summer, after the breeding season, male ducks lose their bright feathers and moult into a dull, brown plumage called eclipse. For a couple of months, from June to September, a drake looks like a female. He is not quite the same: the bill, the eye and the wing are still male, and often some of the colour remains. The four ducks in the first half of the lesson are all in eclipse.\n\nThe second half of the lesson is the sawbills: Goosander, Red-breasted Merganser and Smew. They are fish-eating ducks with thin, serrated bills. The drakes are easy. We look at the females: the grey-bodied ones with a brown head are the ones that turn up in winter on lakes and the coast.\n\nSeason: eclipse is a summer-and-early-autumn plumage, so this lesson fits August to October. Sawbills are winter ducks, from November to March, especially in cold weather."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "eclipse"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The dull, female-like plumage that male ducks wear for a couple of months after breeding. The wing stays male."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "sawbill"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A fish-eating duck (Goosander, merganser, Smew) with a thin bill with tooth-like edges."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crest"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A tuft of long feathers on the head. The female Red-breasted Merganser has a shaggy one."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "chin patch"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A white patch on the chin and throat. A female Goosander has a sharp-edged one."
      }
    }
  ],
